    "beginner_explanation": "Adversary-in-the-Middle is a technique attackers use during the Credential Access, Collection stage of an intrusion. In simple terms: Adversaries may attempt to position themselves between two or more networked devices using an adversary-in-the-middle (AiTM) technique to support follow-on behaviors such as [Network Sniffing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1040), [Transmitted Data Manipulation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1565/002), or replay attacks ([Exploitation for Credential Access](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1212)).",
    "analyst_explanation": "Adversaries may attempt to position themselves between two or more networked devices using an adversary-in-the-middle (AiTM) technique to support follow-on behaviors such as [Network Sniffing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1040), [Transmitted Data Manipulation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1565/002), or replay attacks ([Exploitation for Credential Access](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1212)). By abusing features of common networking protocols that can determine the flow of network traffic (e.g. ARP, DNS, LLMNR, etc.), adversaries may force a device to communicate through ",
    "executive_summary": "Adversary-in-the-Middle (T1557) is a high-severity technique observed in real-world use by 5 known threat actor(s)/malware families, primarily impacting Linux, macOS, Network Devices, Windows.",
    "attacker_view": "An attacker leverages Adversary-in-the-Middle to advance the 'Credential Access' objective, typically requiring access to a Linux, macOS, Network Devices, Windows system.",
    "defender_view": "Defenders should monitor Application Log Content, File Modification, Network Connection Creation and apply mitigations such as Filter Network Traffic, Encrypt Sensitive Information, Limit Access to Resource Over Network to reduce exposure to Adversary-in-the-Middle.",
