    "beginner_explanation": "System Owner/User Discovery is a technique attackers use during the Discovery stage of an intrusion. In simple terms: Adversaries may attempt to identify the primary user, currently logged in user, set of users that commonly uses a system, or whether a user is actively using the system.",
    "analyst_explanation": "Adversaries may attempt to identify the primary user, currently logged in user, set of users that commonly uses a system, or whether a user is actively using the system. They may do this, for example, by retrieving account usernames or by using [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003). The information may be collected in a number of different ways using other Discovery techniques, because user and username details are prevalent throughout a system and include running process ownership, file/directory ownership, session information, and system logs. Adversaries may use",
    "executive_summary": "System Owner/User Discovery (T1033) is a low-severity technique observed in real-world use by 224 known threat actor(s)/malware families, primarily impacting Linux, macOS, Network Devices, Windows.",
    "attacker_view": "An attacker leverages System Owner/User Discovery to advance the 'Discovery' objective, typically requiring access to a Linux, macOS, Network Devices, Windows system.",
    "defender_view": "Defenders should monitor Command Execution, OS API Execution, Process Creation and apply mitigations such as standard hardening controls to reduce exposure to System Owner/User Discovery.",
    "detection_story": "This technique can be surfaced by correlating Behavioral Detection of User Discovery via Local and Remote Enumeration.",
