    "embedding_text": "Pluggable Authentication Modules (T1556.003) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Defense Impairment, Persistence, Credential Access tactic. Adversaries may modify pluggable authentication modules (PAM) to access user credentials or enable otherwise unwarranted access to accounts. PAM is a modular system of configuration files, libraries, and executable files which guide authentication for many services. The most common authentication module is <code>pam_unix.so</code>, which retrieves, sets, and verifies account authentication information in <code>/etc/passwd</code> and <code>/etc/shadow</code>.(Citation: Apple PAM)(Citation: Man Pa It affects Linux, macOS platforms and has been observed used by various threat actors and malware families such as Ebury, Skidmap. Recommended mitigations include Multi-factor Authentication, Privileged Account Management. Detection relies on File Modification, Logon Session Creation, Process Creation.",
    "semantic_summary": "Adversaries may modify pluggable authentication modules (PAM) to access user credentials or enable otherwise unwarranted access to accounts.",
    "llm_context": {
      "id": "T1556.003",
      "name": "Pluggable Authentication Modules",
      "tactics": [
        "Defense Impairment",
        "Persistence",
        "Credential Access"
      ],
      "platforms": [
        "Linux",
        "macOS"
      ],
      "description": "Adversaries may modify pluggable authentication modules (PAM) to access user credentials or enable otherwise unwarranted access to accounts. PAM is a modular system of configuration files, libraries, and executable files which guide authentication for many services. The most common authentication module is <code>pam_unix.so</code>, which retrieves, sets, and verifies account authentication information in <code>/etc/passwd</code> and <code>/etc/shadow</code>.(Citation: Apple PAM)(Citation: Man Pam_Unix)(Citation: Red Hat PAM)\n\nAdversaries may modify components of the PAM system to create backdoors. PAM components, such as <code>pam_unix.so</code>, can be patched to accept arbitrary adversary supplied values as legitimate credentials.(Citation: PAM Backdoor)\n\nMalicious modifications to the PAM system may also be abused to steal credentials. Adversaries may infect PAM resources with code to harvest user credentials, since the values exchanged with PAM components may be plain-text since PAM does not store passwords.(Citation: PAM Creds)(Citation: Apple PAM)",
      "mitigations": [
        "Multi-factor Authentication",
        "Privileged Account Management"
      ],
      "detections": [
        "Detect Malicious Modification of Pluggable Authentication Modules (PAM)"
      ],
      "groups": [],
      "malware": [
        "Ebury",
        "Skidmap"
      ],
      "tools": []
    },
