    "beginner_explanation": "Keylogging is a technique attackers use during the Collection, Credential Access stage of an intrusion. In simple terms: Adversaries may log user keystrokes to intercept credentials as the user types them.",
    "analyst_explanation": "Adversaries may log user keystrokes to intercept credentials as the user types them. Keylogging is likely to be used to acquire credentials for new access opportunities when [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003) efforts are not effective, and may require an adversary to intercept keystrokes on a system for a substantial period of time before credentials can be successfully captured. In order to increase the likelihood of capturing credentials quickly, an adversary may also perform actions such as clearing browser cookies to force users to reauthenticate to systems.",
    "executive_summary": "Keylogging (T1056.001) is a medium-severity technique observed in real-world use by 141 known threat actor(s)/malware families, primarily impacting Linux, macOS, Network Devices, Windows.",
    "attacker_view": "An attacker leverages Keylogging to advance the 'Collection' objective, typically requiring access to a Linux, macOS, Network Devices, Windows system.",
    "defender_view": "Defenders should monitor File Access, Firmware Modification, Network Traffic Content and apply mitigations such as standard hardening controls to reduce exposure to Keylogging.",
    "detection_story": "This technique can be surfaced by correlating Behavioral Detection of Keylogging Activity Across Platforms.",
