    "beginner_explanation": "External Defacement is a technique attackers use during the Impact stage of an intrusion. In simple terms: An adversary may deface systems external to an organization in an attempt to deliver messaging, intimidate, or otherwise mislead an organization or users.",
    "analyst_explanation": "An adversary may deface systems external to an organization in an attempt to deliver messaging, intimidate, or otherwise mislead an organization or users. [External Defacement](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1491/002) may ultimately cause users to distrust the systems and to question/discredit the system’s integrity. Externally-facing websites are a common victim of defacement; often targeted by adversary and hacktivist groups in order to push a political message or spread propaganda.(Citation: FireEye Cyber Threats to Media Industries)(Citation: Kevin Mandia Statement to US Senate Commi",
    "executive_summary": "External Defacement (T1491.002) is a critical-severity technique observed in real-world use by 2 known threat actor(s)/malware families, primarily impacting Windows, IaaS, Linux, macOS.",
    "attacker_view": "An attacker leverages External Defacement to advance the 'Impact' objective, typically requiring access to a Windows, IaaS, Linux, macOS system.",
    "defender_view": "Defenders should monitor Cloud Storage Access, Cloud Storage Enumeration, File Creation and apply mitigations such as Data Backup to reduce exposure to External Defacement.",
