    "embedding_text": "Masquerade File Type (T1036.008) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Stealth tactic. Adversaries may masquerade malicious payloads as legitimate files through changes to the payload's formatting, including the file’s signature, extension, icon, and contents. Various file types have a typical standard format, including how they are encoded and organized. For example, a file’s signature (also known as header or magic bytes) is the beginning bytes of a file and is often used to identify the file’s type. For example, the header of a JPEG file,  is <code> 0xFF 0xD8</code> and the fil It affects Linux, macOS, Windows platforms and has been observed used by BlackByte, MirrorFace, Mustang Panda, Volt Typhoon and malware families such as ANDROMEDA, AvosLocker, HeartCrypt, Kapeka, Lumma Stealer, MagicRAT, OSX_OCEANLOTUS.D, PureCrypter. Recommended mitigations include Behavior Prevention on Endpoint, Antivirus/Antimalware, Execution Prevention. Detection relies on File Creation, File Metadata, Process Creation.",
