    "embedding_text": "Symmetric Cryptography (T1573.001) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Command and Control tactic. Adversaries may employ a known symmetric encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Symmetric encryption algorithms use the same key for plaintext encryption and ciphertext decryption. Common symmetric encryption algorithms include AES, DES, 3DES, Blowfish, and RC4. It affects ESXi, Linux, macOS, Network Devices, Windows platforms and has been observed used by APT28, APT33, BRONZE BUTLER, Contagious Interview, Darkhotel, Higaisa, Inception, Lazarus Group and malware families such as 3PARA RAT, 4H RAT, ADVSTORESHELL, Attor, Azorult, BADCALL, BADNEWS, BBSRAT. Recommended mitigations include Network Intrusion Prevention. Detection relies on Application Log Content, Module Load, Network Connection Creation, Network Traffic Content, Network Traffic Flow.",
    "semantic_summary": "Adversaries may employ a known symmetric encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol.",
