    "embedding_text": "AppDomainManager (T1574.014) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Stealth, Execution tactic. Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking how the .NET `AppDomainManager` loads assemblies. The .NET framework uses the `AppDomainManager` class to create and manage one or more isolated runtime environments (called application domains) inside a process to host the execution of .NET applications. Assemblies (`.exe` or `.dll` binaries compiled to run as .NET code) may be loaded into an application domain as executable code.(Citation: Microsoft App Domains) \n\nKnown as \"AppD It affects Windows platforms and has been observed used by various threat actors and malware families such as IMAPLoader. Recommended mitigations include Restrict File and Directory Permissions. Detection relies on File Creation, Module Load, Process Creation.",
    "semantic_summary": "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking how the .NET `AppDomainManager` loads assemblies.",
