    "embedding_text": "Replication Through Removable Media (T1091) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Lateral Movement, Initial Access tactic. Adversaries may move onto systems, possibly those on disconnected or air-gapped networks, by copying malware to removable media and taking advantage of Autorun features when the media is inserted into a system and executes. In the case of Lateral Movement, this may occur through modification of executable files stored on removable media or by copying malware and renaming it to look like a legitimate file to trick users into executing it on a separate system. In the case of Initial Access, this m It affects Windows platforms and has been observed used by APT28, Aoqin Dragon, Darkhotel, FIN7, Gamaredon Group, LuminousMoth, Mustang Panda, Tropic Trooper and malware families such as ANDROMEDA, Agent.btz, CHOPSTICK, Conficker, Crimson, DustySky, Flame, H1N1. Recommended mitigations include Disable or Remove Feature or Program, Limit Hardware Installation, Behavior Prevention on Endpoint. Detection relies on Drive Creation, File Access, File Creation, Process Creation.",
    "semantic_summary": "Adversaries may move onto systems, possibly those on disconnected or air-gapped networks, by copying malware to removable media and taking advantage of Autorun features when the media is inserted into a system and executes.",
