    "embedding_text": "Application Shimming (T1546.011) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Privilege Escalation, Persistence tactic. Adversaries may establish persistence and/or elevate privileges by executing malicious content triggered by application shims. The Microsoft Windows Application Compatibility Infrastructure/Framework (Application Shim) was created to allow for backward compatibility of software as the operating system codebase changes over time. For example, the application shimming feature allows developers to apply fixes to applications (without rewriting code) that were created for Windows XP so that it will  It affects Windows platforms and has been observed used by FIN7 and malware families such as Pillowmint, SDBbot, ShimRat. Recommended mitigations include User Account Control, Update Software. Detection relies on File Creation, Module Load, Process Creation, Windows Registry Key Modification.",
