    "embedding_text": "DHCP Spoofing (T1557.003) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Credential Access, Collection tactic. Adversaries may redirect network traffic to adversary-owned systems by spoofing Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) traffic and acting as a malicious DHCP server on the victim network. By achieving the adversary-in-the-middle (AiTM) position, adversaries may collect network communications, including passed credentials, especially those sent over insecure, unencrypted protocols. This may also enable follow-on behaviors such as [Network Sniffing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1040) o It affects Linux, Windows, macOS platforms and has been observed used by various threat actors . Recommended mitigations include Filter Network Traffic, Network Intrusion Prevention. Detection relies on Application Log Content, Network Traffic Content, Network Traffic Flow.",
