    "embedding_text": "Password Spraying (T1110.003) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Credential Access tactic. Adversaries may use a single or small list of commonly used passwords against many different accounts to attempt to acquire valid account credentials. Password spraying uses one password (e.g. 'Password01'), or a small list of commonly used passwords, that may match the complexity policy of the domain. Logins are attempted with that password against many different accounts on a network to avoid account lockouts that would normally occur when brute forcing a single account with many passwords. (C It affects Containers, ESXi, IaaS, Identity Provider, Linux, Network Devices, Office Suite, SaaS, Windows, macOS platforms and has been observed used by APT28, APT29, APT33, Agrius, Chimera, Ember Bear, HAFNIUM, HEXANE and malware families such as Bad Rabbit, Linux Rabbit. Recommended mitigations include Multi-factor Authentication, Password Policies, Account Use Policies. Detection relies on User Account Authentication.",
    "semantic_summary": "Adversaries may use a single or small list of commonly used passwords against many different accounts to attempt to acquire valid account credentials.",
