    "beginner_explanation": "Account Discovery is a technique attackers use during the Discovery stage of an intrusion. In simple terms: Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of valid accounts, usernames, or email addresses on a system or within a compromised environment.",
    "analyst_explanation": "Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of valid accounts, usernames, or email addresses on a system or within a compromised environment. This information can help adversaries determine which accounts exist, which can aid in follow-on behavior such as brute-forcing, spear-phishing attacks, or account takeovers (e.g., [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078)).\n\nAdversaries may use several methods to enumerate accounts, including abuse of existing tools, built-in commands, and potential misconfigurations that leak account names and roles or permissions in the targeted environ",
    "executive_summary": "Account Discovery (T1087) is a low-severity technique observed in real-world use by 7 known threat actor(s)/malware families, primarily impacting ESXi, IaaS, Identity Provider, Linux, macOS, Office Suite, SaaS, Windows.",
    "attacker_view": "An attacker leverages Account Discovery to advance the 'Discovery' objective, typically requiring access to a ESXi, IaaS, Identity Provider, Linux, macOS, Office Suite, SaaS, Windows system.",
    "defender_view": "Defenders should monitor Cloud Service Enumeration, Command Execution, File Access and apply mitigations such as Operating System Configuration, User Account Management to reduce exposure to Account Discovery.",
