    "embedding_text": "Steal or Forge Authentication Certificates (T1649) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Credential Access tactic. Adversaries may steal or forge certificates used for authentication to access remote systems or resources. Digital certificates are often used to sign and encrypt messages and/or files. Certificates are also used as authentication material. For example, Entra ID device certificates and Active Directory Certificate Services (AD CS) certificates bind to an identity and can be used as credentials for domain accounts.(Citation: O365 Blog Azure AD Device IDs)(Citation: Microsoft AD CS Overview)\n\nAuth It affects Windows, Linux, macOS, Identity Provider platforms and has been observed used by APT29 . Recommended mitigations include Active Directory Configuration, Disable or Remove Feature or Program, Encrypt Sensitive Information, Audit. Detection relies on Active Directory Credential Request, Active Directory Object Modification, Application Log Content, Command Execution, File Access.",
    "semantic_summary": "Adversaries may steal or forge certificates used for authentication to access remote systems or resources.",
