    "beginner_explanation": "Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder is a technique attackers use during the Persistence, Privilege Escalation stage of an intrusion. In simple terms: Adversaries may achieve persistence by adding a program to a startup folder or referencing it with a Registry run key.",
    "analyst_explanation": "Adversaries may achieve persistence by adding a program to a startup folder or referencing it with a Registry run key. Adding an entry to the \"run keys\" in the Registry or startup folder will cause the program referenced to be executed when a user logs in.(Citation: Microsoft Run Key) These programs will be executed under the context of the user and will have the account's associated permissions level.\n\nThe following run keys are created by default on Windows systems:\n\n* <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run</code>\n* <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\\Software\\Microsoft\\Wi",
    "executive_summary": "Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder (T1547.001) is a medium-severity technique observed in real-world use by 249 known threat actor(s)/malware families, primarily impacting Windows.",
    "attacker_view": "An attacker leverages Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder to advance the 'Persistence' objective, typically requiring access to a Windows system.",
    "defender_view": "Defenders should monitor File Creation, Process Creation, Windows Registry Key Modification and apply mitigations such as standard hardening controls to reduce exposure to Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder.",
    "detection_story": "This technique can be surfaced by correlating Detect Registry and Startup Folder Persistence (Windows).",
