    "embedding_text": "Kernel Modules and Extensions (T1547.006) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Persistence, Privilege Escalation tactic. Adversaries may modify the kernel to automatically execute programs on system boot. Loadable Kernel Modules (LKMs) are pieces of code that can be loaded and unloaded into the kernel upon demand. They extend the functionality of the kernel without the need to reboot the system. For example, one type of module is the device driver, which allows the kernel to access hardware connected to the system.(Citation: Linux Kernel Programming) \n\nWhen used maliciously, LKMs can be a type of kernel-mode [Root It affects macOS, Linux platforms and has been observed used by various threat actors and malware families such as Drovorub, REPTILE, Skidmap. Recommended mitigations include Privileged Account Management, User Account Management, Antivirus/Antimalware, Execution Prevention. Detection relies on Command Execution, File Creation, File Modification, Kernel Module Load, Process Creation.",
