    "embedding_text": "Multi-hop Proxy (T1090.003) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Command and Control tactic. Adversaries may chain together multiple proxies to disguise the source of malicious traffic. Typically, a defender will be able to identify the last proxy traffic traversed before it enters their network; the defender may or may not be able to identify any previous proxies before the last-hop proxy. This technique makes identifying the original source of the malicious traffic even more difficult by requiring the defender to trace malicious traffic through several proxies to identify its source.\n It affects ESXi, Linux, macOS, Network Devices, Windows platforms and has been observed used by APT28, APT29, Ember Bear, FIN4, Gamaredon Group, Inception, Leviathan, Lotus Blossom and malware families such as Attor, BOLDMOVE, Cyclops Blink, Dok, Dridex, GreyEnergy, Industroyer, Keydnap. Recommended mitigations include Filter Network Traffic. Detection relies on Firmware Modification, Network Connection Creation, Network Traffic Content, Network Traffic Flow, Process Creation.",
    "semantic_summary": "Adversaries may chain together multiple proxies to disguise the source of malicious traffic.",
