    "embedding_text": "Encrypted Channel (T1573) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Command and Control tactic. Adversaries may employ an encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Despite the use of a secure algorithm, these implementations may be vulnerable to reverse engineering if secret keys are encoded and/or generated within malware samples/configuration files. It affects ESXi, Linux, macOS, Network Devices, Windows platforms and has been observed used by APT29, BITTER, Magic Hound, Tropic Trooper and malware families such as Chaes, Cryptoistic, Emotet, Gomir, Lizar, MacMa, NETWIRE, PowGoop. Recommended mitigations include Network Intrusion Prevention, SSL/TLS Inspection. Detection relies on Application Log Content, Module Load, Network Connection Creation, Network Traffic Content, Network Traffic Flow.",
    "semantic_summary": "Adversaries may employ an encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol.",
