    "embedding_text": "Bidirectional Communication (T1102.002) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Command and Control tactic. Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service as a means for sending commands to and receiving output from a compromised system over the Web service channel. Compromised systems may leverage popular websites and social media to host command and control (C2) instructions. Those infected systems can then send the output from those commands back over that Web service channel. The return traffic may occur in a variety of ways, depending on the Web service being utilized. For examp It affects ESXi, Linux, macOS, Windows platforms and has been observed used by APT12, APT28, APT37, APT39, Carbanak, FIN7, Gamaredon Group, HEXANE and malware families such as BADNEWS, BLACKCOFFEE, BLUELIGHT, BoxCaon, CALENDAR, Clambling, CloudDuke, ComRAT. Recommended mitigations include Restrict Web-Based Content, Network Intrusion Prevention. Detection relies on Network Connection Creation, Network Traffic Content, Network Traffic Flow, Process Creation.",
    "semantic_summary": "Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service as a means for sending commands to and receiving output from a compromised system over the Web service channel.",
