    "embedding_text": "Asymmetric Cryptography (T1573.002) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Command and Control tactic. Adversaries may employ a known asymmetric encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Asymmetric cryptography, also known as public key cryptography, uses a keypair per party: one public that can be freely distributed, and one private. Due to how the keys are generated, the sender encrypts data with the receiver’s public key and the receiver decrypts the data with their private key. This ensures It affects ESXi, Linux, macOS, Network Devices, Windows platforms and has been observed used by APT42, Cobalt Group, FIN6, FIN8, Medusa Group, OilRig, RedCurl, RedEcho and malware families such as ADVSTORESHELL, Attor, BADHATCH, BISCUIT, BOLDMOVE, BRICKSTORM, Bazar, CASTLETAP. Recommended mitigations include Network Intrusion Prevention, SSL/TLS Inspection. Detection relies on Application Log Content, Module Load, Network Connection Creation, Network Traffic Content, Network Traffic Flow.",
