    "embedding_text": "Protocol or Service Impersonation (T1001.003) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Command and Control tactic. Adversaries may impersonate legitimate protocols or web service traffic to disguise command and control activity and thwart analysis efforts. By impersonating legitimate protocols or web services, adversaries can make their command and control traffic blend in with legitimate network traffic.  \n\nAdversaries may impersonate a fake SSL/TLS handshake to make it look like subsequent traffic is SSL/TLS encrypted, potentially interfering with some security tooling, or to make the traffic look like it  It affects ESXi, Linux, macOS, Windows platforms and has been observed used by Higaisa, Lazarus Group, Mustang Panda and malware families such as BADCALL, BOOKWORM, Bankshot, Cobalt Strike, FALLCHILL, FRAMESTING, FakeM, HARDRAIN. Recommended mitigations include Network Intrusion Prevention. Detection relies on Network Connection Creation, Network Traffic Content, Process Creation, Process Metadata.",
    "semantic_summary": "Adversaries may impersonate legitimate protocols or web service traffic to disguise command and control activity and thwart analysis efforts.",
