    "embedding_text": "ARP Cache Poisoning (T1557.002) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Credential Access, Collection tactic. Adversaries may poison Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) caches to position themselves between the communication of two or more networked devices. This activity may be used to enable follow-on behaviors such as [Network Sniffing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1040) or [Transmitted Data Manipulation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1565/002).\n\nThe ARP protocol is used to resolve IPv4 addresses to link layer addresses, such as a media access control (MAC) address.(Citation: RFC826 ARP) It affects Linux, Windows, macOS platforms and has been observed used by Cleaver, LuminousMoth . Recommended mitigations include Encrypt Sensitive Information, Network Intrusion Prevention, User Training, Disable or Remove Feature or Program, Limit Access to Resource Over Network. Detection relies on Network Connection Creation, Network Traffic Content, Network Traffic Flow.",
