    "embedding_text": "Access Token Manipulation (T1134) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Stealth, Privilege Escalation tactic. Adversaries may modify access tokens to operate under a different user or system security context to perform actions and bypass access controls. Windows uses access tokens to determine the ownership of a running process. A user can manipulate access tokens to make a running process appear as though it is the child of a different process or belongs to someone other than the user that started the process. When this occurs, the process also takes on the security context associated with the new toke It affects Windows platforms and has been observed used by Blue Mockingbird, FIN6, Lotus Blossom and malware families such as AppleSeed, BlackCat, Cuba, Duqu, Gelsemium, HermeticWiper, Hydraq, KillDisk. Recommended mitigations include User Account Management, Privileged Account Management. Detection relies on Active Directory Object Modification, Logon Session Metadata, OS API Execution, Process Access, Process Creation.",
    "semantic_summary": "Adversaries may modify access tokens to operate under a different user or system security context to perform actions and bypass access controls.",
