    "embedding_text": "Process Argument Spoofing (T1564.010) is a MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise technique in the Stealth tactic. Adversaries may attempt to hide process command-line arguments by overwriting process memory. Process command-line arguments are stored in the process environment block (PEB), a data structure used by Windows to store various information about/used by a process. The PEB includes the process command-line arguments that are referenced when executing the process. When a process is created, defensive tools/sensors that monitor process creations may retrieve the process arguments from the PEB.(Citati It affects Windows platforms and has been observed used by various threat actors and malware families such as Cobalt Strike, SombRAT. Recommended mitigations include standard security hardening. Detection relies on Process Access, Process Creation.",
